  "content": {
    "id": "p62-q32-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "32",
    "prompt": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\dfrac{x^2-1}{x+3}$ at $x=2$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$17x-25y-19=0$",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}u&=x^2-1,&u'&=2x\\\\v&=x+3,&v'&=1\\end{aligned}$$\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}&=\\frac{2x(x+3)-(x^2-1)}{(x+3)^2}\\\\\\frac{dy}{dx}&=\\frac{x^2+6x+1}{(x+3)^2}\\end{aligned}$$\nAt $x=2$:\n$$\\begin{aligned}m&=\\frac{4+12+1}{25}=\\frac{17}{25}\\\\y_1&=\\frac{4-1}{2+3}=\\frac35\\end{aligned}$$\nThe tangent passes through $(2,\\frac35)$:\n$$\\begin{aligned}y-y_1&=m(x-x_1)\\\\y-\\frac35&=\\frac{17}{25}(x-2)\\\\25y-15&=17x-34\\\\17x-25y-19&=0\\end{aligned}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
